@head-of-experiments — both points are now tested, and point 1 was worth the test: it is a real hole, fixed below with your array option. Point 2 needs no new state; the repo already answers it, and a test now pins that answer. Everything here is verified on 24bee01 plus the specimen: 102/102, and each claimed killing mutation was run and kills.

**Point 1, fixed.** A disclosure slot now holds one filing or an array of all of them, and the evaluator reads the set. A scalar stays legal, so no existing builder or test changes. That removes the dependence you found: with one object per key, whichever write survived decided between HELD and DEFAULTED — a writer deciding a verdict, which is exactly what this read path exists to prevent. With a set, write order cannot matter. Your collision case (early proof lands first, on-time proof second) now reads HELD in both orders.

`src/commitment.ts`, the type:

```ts
  /**
   * Disclosures the subject published, keyed by the tx hash they refer to
   * (or, for proof-after-close, by the commitment id).
   *
   * A slot holds every filing made under its key, in any order: a scalar is
   * one filing, an array is all of them. The scalar form stays legal so no
   * existing chain-state builder breaks, but ONE SLOT IS NOT ONE FILING. A
   * builder that keeps a single object per key must silently drop or overwrite
   * when a subject files twice -- an early proof and then an on-time one --
   * and which filing survives would then decide the verdict. That choice
   * belongs to no writer: a builder that can see two filings appends both,
   * and the evaluator reads the set.
   */
  disclosures?: Record<string, { at_time: number } | { at_time: number }[]>;
```

`src/evaluate.ts`, a helper beside `inWindow`:

```ts
function filings(slot: { at_time: number } | { at_time: number }[] | undefined): { at_time: number }[] {
  if (slot === undefined) return [];
  return Array.isArray(slot) ? slot : [slot];
}
```

